WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.910
All right. So this is about Doubly-efficient zkSNARKs,

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:04.245
ah, without trusted setup.

00:00:04.245 --> 00:00:06.690
So, ah, zero knowledge succinct, ah,

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:10.065
non- interactive argument of knowledge or a zkSNARK, um,

00:00:10.065 --> 00:00:12.450
these are- these have been the subject of some intense, ah,

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:14.460
research interest and more recently,

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:17.025
even some interest in the broader uh, tech-world.

00:00:17.025 --> 00:00:20.085
So I'm going to tell you about some of the work that my Co-Authors and I did.

00:00:20.085 --> 00:00:23.550
But I'm also going to try and give you a more general picture of the area,

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:25.305
and other, you know, systems in it.

00:00:25.305 --> 00:00:28.695
So first; let's, let's try to cut a little bit through the jargon.

00:00:28.695 --> 00:00:30.870
What is a zkSNARK?

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:33.195
Well, so, it's an argument,

00:00:33.195 --> 00:00:34.500
that is it's a proof,

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:38.100
but one whose validity depends on some cryptographic assumption.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:40.740
Um, and it's an argument of knowledge.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.725
Which means you can use it to prove, roughly speaking,

00:00:43.725 --> 00:00:47.160
that you know a secret and, it's zero knowledge.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:49.980
Meaning that when you use it to prove that you know a secret,

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:52.650
you don't reveal anything about that secret.

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:55.140
Um, now the proof is succinct,

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:58.260
meaning that it's short and it's non-interactive.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:02.295
So it can just be written down and then later somebody can come and read it.

00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:04.785
It doesn't require any kind of conversation.

00:01:04.785 --> 00:01:08.310
And finally, we are concerned about publicly verifiable proofs.

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:10.395
Meaning anyone who reads the proof,

00:01:10.395 --> 00:01:12.645
will be convinced of its validity.

00:01:12.645 --> 00:01:15.210
So, ah, when we compare these different kinds of

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:17.655
SNARKs there are at least five questions that we can ask.

00:01:17.655 --> 00:01:20.220
One is how big is the proof?

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.410
We said it was small, succinct,

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:24.900
but some kinds of zkSNARKs are smaller than others and

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:28.215
generally speaking argument systems may or may not be succinct.

00:01:28.215 --> 00:01:29.630
Proof size is important.

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:32.159
For example, if you're gonna be sending the proofs over a network,

00:01:32.159 --> 00:01:34.950
or if you're going to be storing many proofs for a long time.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:38.240
Another question is, how hard is it to generate a proof?

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:43.035
That is how much time does it take to write down the contents of the proof?

00:01:43.035 --> 00:01:46.440
And relatedly, how much time does it take to check the proof?

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:50.100
Okay? And throughout the talk you're going to see these a script 'P',and script 'V'.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.740
Those refer to the prover, and the verifier.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:56.265
The entity that generates the proof, and the entity that checks the proof.

00:01:56.265 --> 00:02:00.480
Okay. So, remember the validity of these proofs since their arguments really,

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:02.140
depend on some cryptographic assumption.

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:03.750
So another question we can ask is,

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:05.670
what is that cryptographic assumption?

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:08.150
Er, we might care about this because as you would guess the,

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:11.160
the cryptographic community has reason to believe that some assumptions,

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:14.240
are better than others, maybe because they've been battle-tested for longer.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:17.370
And finally, some zkSNARKs including the ones, ah,

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:22.550
that are currently most widely deployed like in Zcash require a trusted setup.

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:24.510
Meaning, that somebody has to run

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:30.270
an expensive one time computation and you can only believe a proof is valid,

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:35.085
if you trust the entity or entities that ran that setup computation.

00:02:35.085 --> 00:02:39.720
So you can see already this is a big design space and every kind of zkSNARK and

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:41.850
every kind of zero knowledge proof system more generally

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:44.550
gives some different mix of these properties.

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:47.220
So the zkSNARK my Co-Authors and I designed it,

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:48.890
we call it Hyrax and we, we,

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:51.150
and it represents sort of a new point in the space.

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:54.570
Hyrax proves the satisfiability of arithmetic circuits.

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:56.940
We'll see what in, what that means in more detail soon.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:59.280
But for now, imagine that we have some computation,

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:02.490
C that's a circuit and that is to say,

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:04.245
you know, sort of has gates in it.

00:03:04.245 --> 00:03:06.240
We'll get into more detail on that soon.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.865
And the prover, convinces the verifier,

00:03:08.865 --> 00:03:12.825
that it knows some value W which we will call the witness,

00:03:12.825 --> 00:03:16.070
that makes the computation or the circuit output true.

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:19.080
Now, for efficiency, Hyrax requires that

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:22.140
the computation has some kind of repetitive structure.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:25.620
For example, it could be many parallel copies of the same computation,

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:27.774
or it could be, repeated instances,

00:03:27.774 --> 00:03:29.715
sort of in series of the computation.

00:03:29.715 --> 00:03:31.530
Again we'll see a little bit more on that later.

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:33.960
Now, in terms of the five costs we just saw,

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:37.620
Hyrax's proofs that are concretely and asymptotically small,

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:40.830
and the prover is concretely fast and asymptotically optimal.

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:42.930
The verifier, is also fast,

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:45.165
ah, and it's slightly work saving,

00:03:45.165 --> 00:03:47.589
which is to say, for big enough circuits,

00:03:47.589 --> 00:03:50.950
the verifier has to do less work to check the circuit.

00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:52.185
Then to check the proof,

00:03:52.185 --> 00:03:53.610
then it would have to, to, to,

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:55.860
sort of check the witness itself.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:04:01.050
Er, and finally, Hyrax relies on a discrete log assumption and it needs no trusted setup.

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:02.640
So these are all sort of,

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:04.950
em, you know, asymptotic statements,

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:06.100
or sort of general statements,

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:09.060
but you might ask sort of more concrete more practical questions,

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:11.100
like, how does it compare to other systems?

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:12.570
And so that's what we did. We ran,

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:14.790
em, several other, em,

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:18.645
SNARK systems and we sort of did a concrete comparison.

00:04:18.645 --> 00:04:21.780
And I'll tell you more about this later but the upshot is,

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:24.600
that Hyrax gives small proofs in the sense that,

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:26.354
if you wanted them to be smaller,

00:04:26.354 --> 00:04:28.860
it seems right now like you would have to pay more in terms

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.845
of prover or verifier computation or both.

00:04:31.845 --> 00:04:33.720
And, Hyrax is fast.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:36.965
Meaning, if you want the prover or verifier to get faster,

00:04:36.965 --> 00:04:40.005
it seems like you have to accept bigger proofs.

00:04:40.005 --> 00:04:41.940
So, that's not to say,

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:44.595
that Hyrax is anything like the best proof system.

00:04:44.595 --> 00:04:46.935
It's not. Because there isn't a best one.

00:04:46.935 --> 00:04:49.525
This isn't a competition and there isn't a winner.

00:04:49.525 --> 00:04:51.565
Right? This is a design space,

00:04:51.565 --> 00:04:55.380
and there are different applications that might use designs in this space.

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:58.110
So when you go shopping for a zero knowledge proof system,

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:00.265
decide what is important to you,

00:05:00.265 --> 00:05:01.630
and choose the correct system.

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:03.035
The system that matches up with that.

00:05:03.035 --> 00:05:05.090
The best. So that's what we're going to do.

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:08.165
We're going to start by taking a look at some of the,

00:05:08.165 --> 00:05:11.330
the design space of general purpose zero knowledge proof systems.

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:13.475
And later we'll look more at Hyrax's design,

00:05:13.475 --> 00:05:16.010
and then some of the evaluation results I mentioned.

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:19.369
So when we talk about general purpose proof systems,

00:05:19.369 --> 00:05:22.820
usually we mean proof systems for NP languages.

00:05:22.820 --> 00:05:25.265
So what that means is there is some relation R,

00:05:25.265 --> 00:05:29.105
and we can think of that as a computation that takes some values,

00:05:29.105 --> 00:05:30.920
and then returns True or False.

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:33.530
And the values, are an input X,

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:36.570
from the verifier, and a witness W, from the approver.

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:38.630
And we can think of the witness as a secret,

00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:41.480
that the prover knows and R the relation as

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:44.895
a computation that checks whether the witness is somehow,

00:05:44.895 --> 00:05:47.535
correct for the verifier's input.

00:05:47.535 --> 00:05:49.070
And at a very high level,

00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:51.590
these systems all operate by turning

00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:55.505
the witness checking computation into a sort of two different computations.

00:05:55.505 --> 00:05:58.385
One, that's the verifier's job,

00:05:58.385 --> 00:06:00.410
and one, that's the prover's job.

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:02.270
So we can think of this as sort of happening in

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:05.945
two stages which we'll call the front end and the back end.

00:06:05.945 --> 00:06:07.520
Now in the front end,

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:11.640
we convert the witness checking computation into an arithmetic circuit.

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:14.195
Which is to say a generalized Boolean circuit,

00:06:14.195 --> 00:06:16.430
where we can think of AND Gates as becoming

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:19.165
multiplication and OR Gates as becoming addition.

00:06:19.165 --> 00:06:22.820
And the front end constructs this arithmetic circuit such that

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:25.340
its satisfiability is tantamount to

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:29.080
the correct execution of the witness checking computation.

00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:32.800
Okay? Then the back end, applies proving machinery,

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:36.830
which is some combination of complexity theoretic and cryptographic objects,

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:39.680
to produce approver and a verifier computation.

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:44.480
Em, so such that a valid proof establishes that the circuit is satisfied.

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:46.535
Which by any implication in the front end

00:06:46.535 --> 00:06:50.710
establishes that the witness checking computation was run correctly.

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:52.030
So up to this point,

00:06:52.030 --> 00:06:55.170
I've been talking about different kinds of zero knowledge proof systems and,

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:57.130
and really what I've been referring to is that,

00:06:57.130 --> 00:06:59.200
that proof machinery in the back end.

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:01.600
So we're going to focus in a little bit more on that.

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:04.505
Ah, let's take a look at how some of the existing systems work.

00:07:04.505 --> 00:07:08.960
So maybe the most well known zero knowledge proof systems are Pinocchio and Libsnark.

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:10.130
These are used for example,

00:07:10.130 --> 00:07:11.600
as I said earlier, in Zcash.

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:16.835
Now these are built on an object called a linear PCP and very roughly speaking,

00:07:16.835 --> 00:07:19.850
their properties are such that they give short proofs,

00:07:19.850 --> 00:07:22.470
and very fast verification, but,

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:26.750
proving is slow, and they rely on cryptographic assumptions that are non-standard.

00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:29.330
That we don't, we don't trust them maybe quite as much.

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:31.490
And finally, they require a trusted setup.

00:07:31.490 --> 00:07:33.865
Somebody has to do that initial computation.

00:07:33.865 --> 00:07:35.420
Now earlier today, ah,

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:38.600
you saw a talk by Benedikt on bullet proofs and, em,

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:43.400
this relies essentially on a different kind of linear PCP and some different crypto and

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:48.280
the result is that bullet proof gives short proofs and unlike Libsnark and Pinocchio,

00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:50.540
it uses standard cryptographic assumptions,

00:07:50.540 --> 00:07:52.205
and it requires no setup.

00:07:52.205 --> 00:07:54.630
On the other hand, em, there's a little price,

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:56.280
and that is that the,

00:07:56.280 --> 00:08:00.580
the proving and veri- especially the verifying gets a little bit slower.

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:04.000
An entirely different class of proof machinery comes from

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:07.430
multi party computation in the head which is due to [inaudible].

00:08:07.430 --> 00:08:14.260
And ZKBoo and ZKB++ are sort of the first practical implementations of this paradigm.

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:16.110
And they have fast proving,

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:18.609
they build on a standard cryptographic assumption,

00:08:18.609 --> 00:08:20.405
and they have no trusted setup.

00:08:20.405 --> 00:08:24.895
But they give long proofs and verifying is slowish.

00:08:24.895 --> 00:08:28.015
What does that mean? Well the asymptotics aren't great.

00:08:28.015 --> 00:08:29.985
So, and they kick in, sort of,

00:08:29.985 --> 00:08:32.080
even for moderately sized problems.

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:34.015
So, for very small computations,

00:08:34.015 --> 00:08:38.750
these are blindingly fast but for bigger computations maybe think again.

00:08:38.750 --> 00:08:44.740
So more recently Ligero improves on the approach from ZKBoo and ZKB++.

00:08:44.740 --> 00:08:46.520
And it uses some different sort of

00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:50.930
complexity theoretic objects to give shorter proofs and faster verification.

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:55.145
I still say -ish here because compared to Libsnark and bulletproofs,

00:08:55.145 --> 00:08:58.215
um the proofs are still longer.

00:08:58.215 --> 00:09:00.245
And compared to Libsnark, the,

00:09:00.245 --> 00:09:02.645
the verifying is still much slower.

00:09:02.645 --> 00:09:06.550
Okay and finally, there's this newest class of machinery.

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:10.625
In some sense building on the oldest class of theory.

00:09:10.625 --> 00:09:13.050
The Short PCPs and

00:09:13.050 --> 00:09:16.320
LibSTARK is right now the only practical implementation of this approach.

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:21.505
And it gives short proofs and fast verification and it needs no trusted setup.

00:09:21.505 --> 00:09:25.105
But proving is slow and it relies on

00:09:25.105 --> 00:09:30.495
an unproven conjecture that has some relation to the hardness of Reed-Solomon decoding.

00:09:30.495 --> 00:09:33.400
So this table is not gospel,

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:36.240
it's just a rough approximation of the state of play.

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:38.435
But the important thing to get from it is,

00:09:38.435 --> 00:09:40.810
that there are all these different systems that you might pick

00:09:40.810 --> 00:09:43.865
depending on which of the columns is most important to you.

00:09:43.865 --> 00:09:47.605
And none of them really is clearly best among these.

00:09:47.605 --> 00:09:50.310
Right. Okay so with that landscape in mind,

00:09:50.310 --> 00:09:52.655
let's take a quick look at Hyrax's machinery.

00:09:52.655 --> 00:09:55.495
So Hyrax is built on a completely different thing called

00:09:55.495 --> 00:09:59.725
an interactive proof which is essentially a probabilistic cross-examination.

00:09:59.725 --> 00:10:01.975
And we make these interactive proofs zero knowledge

00:10:01.975 --> 00:10:04.905
using techniques of Ben-Or et al and Cramer and Damgård.

00:10:04.905 --> 00:10:09.585
But as we'll see naively applying those techniques results in poor performance.

00:10:09.585 --> 00:10:14.300
So in Hyrax we also include several refinements which result in order of magnitudes

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:16.620
improvement in both verifier time and proof

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:20.185
size and small constant factors and improvement for the prover as well.

00:10:20.185 --> 00:10:22.950
So the high level idea in Hyrax is,

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:24.770
that in an interactive proof,

00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:28.765
the verifier sending questions and the prover is sending answers to those questions.

00:10:28.765 --> 00:10:32.230
And what we're gonna do is replace all the answers that the prover

00:10:32.230 --> 00:10:35.895
sends with cryptographic commitments to the answers.

00:10:35.895 --> 00:10:38.300
And then the prover is gonna help the verifier,

00:10:38.300 --> 00:10:42.985
um to check that the contents of the commitment are actually

00:10:42.985 --> 00:10:45.230
a message that would have been valid in the sort of

00:10:45.230 --> 00:10:48.735
underlying interactive proof without revealing anything else.

00:10:48.735 --> 00:10:50.570
Um, so to review,

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:52.430
let's take a look at a cryptographic commitment.

00:10:52.430 --> 00:10:55.900
So in a cryptographic commitment scheme we have a sender that computes

00:10:55.900 --> 00:10:59.515
a commitment to a message and then it sends the commitment to a receiver.

00:10:59.515 --> 00:11:02.689
The later the sender can open the commitment um,

00:11:02.689 --> 00:11:07.565
and then reveal the message inside and convince the receiver that

00:11:07.565 --> 00:11:12.565
the message that's revealed is consistent with the com- with the- with the um,

00:11:12.565 --> 00:11:14.555
um commitment that was sent earlier.

00:11:14.555 --> 00:11:18.140
So generally speaking, commitment schemes have two properties that we care about.

00:11:18.140 --> 00:11:21.460
Hiding means that the commitment reveals nothing about the message

00:11:21.460 --> 00:11:26.645
inside and binding means that the sender can't lie when it tries to open that commitment.

00:11:26.645 --> 00:11:29.680
It can't decide, well I really wanted to send this other message,

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:32.375
so I'm gonna do that instead because it'll get caught cheating.

00:11:32.375 --> 00:11:37.035
So Hyrax uses special class of commitments that have sort of a third, sorry,

00:11:37.035 --> 00:11:39.965
they have sort of a third property a linear homomorphism,

00:11:39.965 --> 00:11:41.975
which means there's some operator that we can

00:11:41.975 --> 00:11:44.315
use essentially to add two commitments together.

00:11:44.315 --> 00:11:45.450
So when we do that,

00:11:45.450 --> 00:11:48.040
we get a new commitment that has inside of it,

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:50.330
a message that's the sum of the two.

00:11:50.330 --> 00:11:52.500
And from that you can get sort of scaling by a- by

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:57.000
some constant factor because you can just add the same message to itself many times.

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:00.415
Okay, so the other thing that Hyrax builds on is an interactive proof

00:12:00.415 --> 00:12:03.485
and specifically one due to Goldwasser, Kalai and Rothblum.

00:12:03.485 --> 00:12:07.250
This is an interactive proof for layered arithmetic circuit evaluation,

00:12:07.250 --> 00:12:10.365
which means that the inputs to the circuit feed one set of

00:12:10.365 --> 00:12:13.765
gates and those- that the outputs from those gates feed a second set,

00:12:13.765 --> 00:12:15.845
the outputs from those feed a third et cetera.

00:12:15.845 --> 00:12:20.530
Um, so to start the proof the verifier gives some inputs to the prover and then

00:12:20.530 --> 00:12:25.875
the prover is gonna evaluate the arithmetic circuit and produce some output y,

00:12:25.875 --> 00:12:27.855
that it's gonna send to the verifier.

00:12:27.855 --> 00:12:30.725
Now the verifier and the prover start to interact.

00:12:30.725 --> 00:12:33.090
So first the verifier is gonna construct

00:12:33.090 --> 00:12:36.815
a polynomial that relates the purported output that's y,

00:12:36.815 --> 00:12:39.065
to the last layer of gates in the circuit.

00:12:39.065 --> 00:12:42.585
Then it's gonna engage the prover in what's called a sum-check protocol.

00:12:42.585 --> 00:12:45.265
This is sort of a foundational interactive proof.

00:12:45.265 --> 00:12:48.410
And the result of that sum-check protocol is

00:12:48.410 --> 00:12:52.025
a claim about the second last layer of the circuit.

00:12:52.025 --> 00:12:54.140
Now in some sense that's not good enough

00:12:54.140 --> 00:12:56.370
because the verifier can't check that very easily.

00:12:56.370 --> 00:12:58.180
So what do the verifier and prover do?

00:12:58.180 --> 00:12:59.630
They-they continue to interact.

00:12:59.630 --> 00:13:03.165
So now we run another sum-check and we get a claim without the prior layer.

00:13:03.165 --> 00:13:05.845
And we keep doing that and eventually we get

00:13:05.845 --> 00:13:09.670
a claim whose truth is related to the inputs to the computation.

00:13:09.670 --> 00:13:13.470
Now in a non-zero knowledge version of GKR, this would be it,

00:13:13.470 --> 00:13:15.210
we would be done because the verifier has

00:13:15.210 --> 00:13:18.020
those inputs and it can just check that claim directly.

00:13:18.020 --> 00:13:21.980
But in Zero knowledge we need to make two modifications.

00:13:21.980 --> 00:13:23.815
So the first one as I said earlier,

00:13:23.815 --> 00:13:27.070
is that the prover  is going to replace all of its messages in

00:13:27.070 --> 00:13:30.640
the sum-check invocations with commitments to those messages.

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:33.640
And the second, and this one's a little more complicated,

00:13:33.640 --> 00:13:39.520
is that we need to give the verifier some way to check that final statement in the proof.

00:13:39.520 --> 00:13:43.500
And remember, the verifier never gets to see the witness.

00:13:43.500 --> 00:13:45.980
And since this is a witness checking computation,

00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:48.215
somehow the inputs have to encode the witness.

00:13:48.215 --> 00:13:50.850
So the verifier certainly can't check that directly.

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:53.130
So what's it gonna do? Well, we're going to use what we

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:55.925
call a polynomial commitment scheme.

00:13:55.925 --> 00:13:58.650
So the the final check that the verifier needs to

00:13:58.650 --> 00:14:02.275
do is just evaluating a polynomial that we'll call tilde m.

00:14:02.275 --> 00:14:06.815
And this polynomial encodes both the input and the witness.

00:14:06.815 --> 00:14:08.455
Now to run that check,

00:14:08.455 --> 00:14:14.540
the prover will send a commitment to the verifier at the start of the protocol.

00:14:14.540 --> 00:14:16.360
So before any interaction happens.

00:14:16.360 --> 00:14:19.000
Okay then the prover and the verifier are gonna run

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:21.785
the interactive proof that we just saw on the previous slide.

00:14:21.785 --> 00:14:24.900
And at the end the prover is just going to make a claim, it's going to say,

00:14:24.900 --> 00:14:28.965
"here the evaluation that you really want is this one, is this value here."

00:14:28.965 --> 00:14:33.310
And finally the prover and the verifier are going to engage in an- in

00:14:33.310 --> 00:14:35.490
an additional protocol that convinces

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:39.070
the verifier that the output that the prover just claimed,

00:14:39.070 --> 00:14:41.255
is consistent with the commitments

00:14:41.255 --> 00:14:44.075
that the prover sent at the beginning of the protocol.

00:14:44.075 --> 00:14:46.665
So um, Hyrax uses

00:14:46.665 --> 00:14:51.230
a new polynomial commitment scheme for multi linear polynomials which is what m tilde is.

00:14:51.230 --> 00:14:53.930
And I'm going to show you a little bit about how that works now.

00:14:53.930 --> 00:14:57.210
So these multilinear polynomials have a specific form.

00:14:57.210 --> 00:15:00.405
Namely, we can think of them as a vector matrix, vector product.

00:15:00.405 --> 00:15:03.390
So the vectors L and R depend only on

00:15:03.390 --> 00:15:07.100
the point at which we evaluate the polynomial and the Matrix T

00:15:07.100 --> 00:15:14.025
encodes the monomials of tilde M. In our case the matrix is encoding W the witness.

00:15:14.025 --> 00:15:17.450
So a naive approach is that we would just build

00:15:17.450 --> 00:15:20.105
a polynomial commitment scheme by having the prover commit

00:15:20.105 --> 00:15:22.965
to each element of the Matrix T individually.

00:15:22.965 --> 00:15:24.345
And I think- if you think about it,

00:15:24.345 --> 00:15:26.555
you'll see that- that's enough already because

00:15:26.555 --> 00:15:30.275
since then- these commitments as I said earlier have this linear homomorphism,

00:15:30.275 --> 00:15:36.230
we can already compute a vector matrix vector product just by using that homomorphism.

00:15:36.230 --> 00:15:37.760
Okay, but that's inefficient.

00:15:37.760 --> 00:15:41.630
Why? Well the commitment size and the work to do

00:15:41.630 --> 00:15:43.940
the polynomial- to compute the commitment to

00:15:43.940 --> 00:15:47.000
the polynomial is linear in the size of the witness.

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:48.560
Right. So as the witness gets bigger,

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:51.330
it scales linearly and we'd rather scale sub linearly.

00:15:51.330 --> 00:15:53.595
Um, so to sidestep that problem,

00:15:53.595 --> 00:15:56.680
we're going to follow an idea due to Jens Groth who uses

00:15:56.680 --> 00:16:00.785
multi commitment schemes to commit to each row of the matrix.

00:16:00.785 --> 00:16:02.230
So what's a multi commitment?

00:16:02.230 --> 00:16:05.935
Well it's a commitment where the message inside is a vector.

00:16:05.935 --> 00:16:09.230
And if we use the Pedersen commitment then what we

00:16:09.230 --> 00:16:12.690
get is a multi commitment that has a vector wise homomorphism.

00:16:12.690 --> 00:16:14.105
So if we have two commitments,

00:16:14.105 --> 00:16:16.940
two vectors and we use the add- addition operator on them,

00:16:16.940 --> 00:16:18.950
what we get is a commitment to a new vector that's

00:16:18.950 --> 00:16:22.180
the sum vector wise of the previous two.

00:16:22.180 --> 00:16:24.830
Okay, so then how are we gonna use this?

00:16:24.830 --> 00:16:28.115
Well, the verifier is gonna compute the vector matrix product,

00:16:28.115 --> 00:16:29.600
L times T, uh,

00:16:29.600 --> 00:16:32.250
by itself using that vector-wise homomorphism.

00:16:32.250 --> 00:16:34.415
And I thi- and again, I think we can see how that happens.

00:16:34.415 --> 00:16:38.515
The entries of L are each scaling one row of the Matrix T,

00:16:38.515 --> 00:16:41.240
and then we sum those scaled versions together.

00:16:41.240 --> 00:16:43.905
And then second, the prover is gonna send

00:16:43.905 --> 00:16:47.950
a commitment to a claimed evaluation of tilde M,

00:16:47.950 --> 00:16:50.280
and then finally the prover is gonna use what's

00:16:50.280 --> 00:16:52.670
called the dot product argument or an inner product argument,

00:16:52.670 --> 00:16:55.740
to convince the verifier that the claimed evaluation is

00:16:55.740 --> 00:17:00.110
consistent with the vector R and the vector matrix product L times T,

00:17:00.110 --> 00:17:02.085
that the verifier previously computed.

00:17:02.085 --> 00:17:04.380
Now, if this sounds familiar it's because you've

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:06.750
already seen this earlier today in Bulletproofs.

00:17:06.750 --> 00:17:10.725
In fact, we adapt the dot product argument from Bulletproofs um,

00:17:10.725 --> 00:17:12.155
and the result is that,

00:17:12.155 --> 00:17:13.370
um, this has some, er,

00:17:13.370 --> 00:17:16.230
kind of very low cost in terms of the communication.

00:17:16.230 --> 00:17:18.550
Specifically, it's logarithmic in the length in,

00:17:18.550 --> 00:17:21.490
in sort of the number of columns in the matrix.

00:17:21.490 --> 00:17:24.585
Um, so that means if the matrix is a square,

00:17:24.585 --> 00:17:27.370
then the prover is gonna send one commitment per row,

00:17:27.370 --> 00:17:28.770
plus a logarithmic number of

00:17:28.770 --> 00:17:31.595
extra messages that we don't need to worry too much about, they're small.

00:17:31.595 --> 00:17:33.760
And the verifier's computation is gonna be

00:17:33.760 --> 00:17:37.265
proportional to the sum of the number of rows and the number of columns.

00:17:37.265 --> 00:17:40.375
And in total, both of these are just sort of square root-ish.

00:17:40.375 --> 00:17:45.080
Okay. But alternatively, what we can do is we can adjust the size of the matrix.

00:17:45.080 --> 00:17:48.045
We can make it sort of fewer rows and more columns.

00:17:48.045 --> 00:17:52.475
And what that means, is that the prover ends up sending less information to the verifier,

00:17:52.475 --> 00:17:55.940
and then the verifier ends up having to do a little more work to,

00:17:55.940 --> 00:18:00.130
uh, to sort of compute over the columns of the matrix.

00:18:00.130 --> 00:18:01.875
What this means at the,

00:18:01.875 --> 00:18:05.545
at the end is that you can tailor Hyrax to your applications' needs.

00:18:05.545 --> 00:18:07.540
If you need less communication,

00:18:07.540 --> 00:18:11.790
you can pay for it with a little bit more verification time.

00:18:11.790 --> 00:18:15.140
Okay, so I've described everything in terms of an interactive proof.

00:18:15.140 --> 00:18:16.470
To get it non-interactive,

00:18:16.470 --> 00:18:17.960
we use the Fiat-Shamir heuristic.

00:18:17.960 --> 00:18:21.060
Um, and w- we give several other refinements that I'll leave to the paper,

00:18:21.060 --> 00:18:22.480
but I'll just mention them quickly here.

00:18:22.480 --> 00:18:25.230
First, we also use these multi commitments, um,

00:18:25.230 --> 00:18:28.945
to save proof size and verifier work in the sum check invocations.

00:18:28.945 --> 00:18:31.800
And second, we develop what we call a redistribution layer,

00:18:31.800 --> 00:18:32.900
which remember I said,

00:18:32.900 --> 00:18:36.035
we need some sort of repeated structure to the computation.

00:18:36.035 --> 00:18:39.590
The point of the redistribution layer is to sort of give us a wider range of

00:18:39.590 --> 00:18:43.900
computations that we can turn into something that has the appropriate repeated structure.

00:18:43.900 --> 00:18:46.560
And finally, we tweak the giraffe interactive proof

00:18:46.560 --> 00:18:49.030
which is prior work to make gi- Giraffe plus,

00:18:49.030 --> 00:18:52.795
plus which reduces the proof size somewhat further.

00:18:52.795 --> 00:18:57.175
Okay, so let's take a look at the evaluation results.

00:18:57.175 --> 00:19:00.680
So, uh, our points of comparison are five existing systems.

00:19:00.680 --> 00:19:04.050
That's BCCGP square root, Bulletproofs, ZKB++,

00:19:04.050 --> 00:19:08.400
Ligero, LibSTARK, and then also two versions of Hyrax.

00:19:08.400 --> 00:19:13.050
One, where the, the matrix T has sort of twice as many columns

00:19:13.050 --> 00:19:17.685
as rows and we use the polynomial commitment scheme trick that I described earlier,

00:19:17.685 --> 00:19:20.280
and the other where we leave off all the refinements.

00:19:20.280 --> 00:19:22.450
We just do them the naive straightforward thing.

00:19:22.450 --> 00:19:25.145
So, the idea there is let's see how much those refinements help.

00:19:25.145 --> 00:19:28.295
And don't worry too much about memorizing the legend,

00:19:28.295 --> 00:19:30.020
I will repeat it on the next lines, I promise.

00:19:30.020 --> 00:19:34.120
Um, so we compare these systems at 90-bit security which

00:19:34.120 --> 00:19:35.780
arguably is too low but maybe is sort of

00:19:35.780 --> 00:19:38.580
the starting point for what you might use for a non-interactive proof.

00:19:38.580 --> 00:19:42.630
Um, and the benchmark I'm gonna show you is what's called a SHA-256 Merkle tree.

00:19:42.630 --> 00:19:44.870
So, in this benchmark, the prover is gonna convince

00:19:44.870 --> 00:19:48.385
the verifier that it knows all the leaves of a hash tree,

00:19:48.385 --> 00:19:51.770
that correspond to a root that the verifier holds.

00:19:51.770 --> 00:19:58.190
Okay, so first up is proof size versus log of the number of leaves in the hash tree.

00:19:58.190 --> 00:20:01.130
So, both X and Y axes are effectively logarithmic.

00:20:01.130 --> 00:20:03.725
Um, and the green dots are Hyrax.

00:20:03.725 --> 00:20:06.975
So remember Hyrax lets you tailor the proof size in the verifier time.

00:20:06.975 --> 00:20:08.290
And as I said earlier,

00:20:08.290 --> 00:20:09.605
we tailored it to sort of,

00:20:09.605 --> 00:20:12.835
um, a sho- a, a, short fat matrix.

00:20:12.835 --> 00:20:15.260
So, what I'm showing you here I call Hyrax one-third.

00:20:15.260 --> 00:20:18.570
Um, and the result of that sort of tailoring is

00:20:18.570 --> 00:20:22.175
that the Hyrax has kind of the second best proof size among these systems.

00:20:22.175 --> 00:20:26.365
But, you can see that Bulletproofs has mu- much much better proof size, much smaller.

00:20:26.365 --> 00:20:28.405
And on the other side, ZKBoo,

00:20:28.405 --> 00:20:30.965
the red squares, has these very large proofs,

00:20:30.965 --> 00:20:33.265
and the naive version of Hyrax where we don't apply

00:20:33.265 --> 00:20:35.790
any of the optimizations is, is even worse, right?

00:20:35.790 --> 00:20:38.245
It's huge, huge proofs. Um, okay.

00:20:38.245 --> 00:20:40.545
But what that means is, is in some sense the refinements are,

00:20:40.545 --> 00:20:41.720
are working already, right?

00:20:41.720 --> 00:20:43.370
We can see that they improve the proof size.

00:20:43.370 --> 00:20:47.195
Next up is a prover time.

00:20:47.195 --> 00:20:49.260
Again, this is log, log scale.

00:20:49.260 --> 00:20:52.160
Um, and remember ZKB++,

00:20:52.160 --> 00:20:53.440
remember you'll see it, it had

00:20:53.440 --> 00:20:56.590
the biggest proofs but now it also has the fastest prover, right?

00:20:56.590 --> 00:20:59.520
So, if you need a screaming fast prover, you choose ZKB.

00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:01.000
Uh, Ligero's prover, uh,

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:02.890
also has a, has a good showing.

00:21:02.890 --> 00:21:05.055
Um, and then Hyrax is kind of next up.

00:21:05.055 --> 00:21:10.125
And what you see is that whereas Bulletproofs had sort of a very small proof,

00:21:10.125 --> 00:21:15.010
you pay for that by having to sort of work harder to generate that proof, right?

00:21:15.010 --> 00:21:18.200
And this is sort of a theme that we'll see a couple more times.

00:21:18.200 --> 00:21:20.005
And finally, you can see there's sort of

00:21:20.005 --> 00:21:23.920
a small constant factor maybe 3X between the blue stars,

00:21:23.920 --> 00:21:25.960
that's the unrefined version of Hyrax and

00:21:25.960 --> 00:21:29.845
the green diamonds or the green hexes, the, uh, refined version.

00:21:29.845 --> 00:21:32.185
Finally, let's look at the verifier time.

00:21:32.185 --> 00:21:36.765
So, here LibSTARK has far and away the best performance and Ligero again,

00:21:36.765 --> 00:21:38.355
very good performance and speed.

00:21:38.355 --> 00:21:40.905
Um, for big enough problems,

00:21:40.905 --> 00:21:43.240
the Hyrax is faster than ZKB++.

00:21:43.240 --> 00:21:45.025
So, remember what I said the,

00:21:45.025 --> 00:21:49.610
the verifier in ZKB is kind of slow-ish or fast-ish depending on how we think about it,

00:21:49.610 --> 00:21:51.505
because once the problem gets big enough,

00:21:51.505 --> 00:21:54.255
then the verifier time starts to run away.

00:21:54.255 --> 00:21:58.910
And finally, this is another case where the improvements from, uh, from Hyrax,

00:21:58.910 --> 00:22:03.390
th- the difference between the blue stars and the green hexes is really big.

00:22:03.390 --> 00:22:07.180
So, those refinements really help in terms of the verifier time.

00:22:07.230 --> 00:22:09.520
Okay, so let's conclude.

00:22:09.520 --> 00:22:13.290
I've shown you Hyrax which is sort of a new general purpose ZKSNARK.

00:22:13.290 --> 00:22:14.975
And as we saw,

00:22:14.975 --> 00:22:16.880
Hyrax gives you kind of small proofs,

00:22:16.880 --> 00:22:18.955
in the sense that if you want them to be smaller,

00:22:18.955 --> 00:22:22.155
you gotta use a system with more prover and verifier cost.

00:22:22.155 --> 00:22:24.905
And it's fast in the sense that if you want faster,

00:22:24.905 --> 00:22:27.345
you have to pay with for that in bigger proofs.

00:22:27.345 --> 00:22:30.260
But, again the most important takeaway here is that

00:22:30.260 --> 00:22:33.305
there's no real winner because there's no actual competition.

00:22:33.305 --> 00:22:37.020
Di- different proof systems give different combinations of properties,

00:22:37.020 --> 00:22:40.785
and what we should be doing is thinking about our application carefully,

00:22:40.785 --> 00:22:42.945
and then choosing the one that applies.

00:22:42.945 --> 00:22:44.895
So Hyrax, from that point of view,

00:22:44.895 --> 00:22:47.730
is just a point in the design space and of course there are

00:22:47.730 --> 00:22:51.240
other points in the design space that are even better that we haven't reached yet.

00:22:51.240 --> 00:22:53.325
So there's plenty more work to do.

00:22:53.325 --> 00:22:57.340
Um, our paper and our code are available online and I hope you'll check them out.

00:22:57.340 --> 00:22:59.220
And with that, I'll take any questions.

00:22:59.220 --> 00:23:04.690
[APPLAUSE].

00:23:04.690 --> 00:23:06.295
Any uh, any questions?

00:23:06.295 --> 00:23:10.120
So, wh- why do you think there isn't gonna be

00:23:10.120 --> 00:23:13.465
a proof system that just dominates everybody else in all coordinates?

00:23:13.465 --> 00:23:15.055
Maybe that, that might happen?

00:23:15.055 --> 00:23:18.250
Um, I think we, we should hope for it in the sense that it would make our lives easy.

00:23:18.250 --> 00:23:21.560
We just run that proof system and be done and then everyone could concentrate on,

00:23:21.560 --> 00:23:23.670
on improving it and,

00:23:23.670 --> 00:23:25.390
and making a nice implementation.

00:23:25.390 --> 00:23:27.335
But, er, you know, it's slightly,

00:23:27.335 --> 00:23:30.925
I think for now magical to hope for it in the sense that everyone is, is,

00:23:30.925 --> 00:23:33.840
using certain tricks to get their good points,

00:23:33.840 --> 00:23:35.120
and in the, and in the end,

00:23:35.120 --> 00:23:36.855
that's what's producing their bad points.

